# .release-removals — deliberate deletions against the PUBLISHED tree.
#
# One path or path-prefix per line; `#` comments allowed. A release that removes a file
# already on public `master` is blocked unless it is declared here. A deletion is not
# forbidden — it must be intended, and this file is the reviewed record that it was.
#
# A comment-only or blank file is NOT an allow-all; it declares nothing.

# ── [ADR-0031], 2026-08-23 — the DATED status snapshots are not a publication surface ──
#
# READ THIS BEFORE READING THE PATH BELOW: `docs/status/STATUS.md` is not being withdrawn
# from the public surface. It MOVED to `docs/STATUS.md` and is declared in
# CANONICAL-DOCS.toml at its new path, in the same commit as this line. Against the
# published tree a move is a deletion plus an addition, and `public-regress` only sees the
# deletion half — so it has to be declared here, and the declaration would otherwise read
# as "arch took its status doc down," which is the opposite of what happened.
#
# What IS withdrawn: everything else under `docs/status/` — 163 dated
# STATUS-*/ROUTING-*/HANDOFF-*/CHECKPOINT-*/AUDIT-* snapshots, plus `WORKSTREAMS.md`. Those
# are working memory: dated measurements, seat names, blockers, half-verified numbers,
# cohort routing packets that name other teams. None of it was ever on the keep-list, so in
# practice none of it has been reaching public `master` — measured 2026-08-23, public
# `master` carries 88 files of which `docs/` holds exactly one. This declaration makes that
# boundary explicit rather than incidental.
#
# Why a PREFIX and not the one file: the separation now runs by path rather than by
# filename, so `docs/status/` can be dropped wholesale and the canonical log is never in the
# blast radius. That is the durable half of the fix.
#
# Arch's own numbers, since this repo was the worst instance in the fleet's measurement:
# the rolling log carried 75 unique commit citations and 61 of them sat in twelve
# accumulated `_Prior:` entries and a 145-line July narrative — all `dev` SHAs that by
# [ADR-0027] have never resolved for a public reader. The history moved verbatim to
# `docs/status/STATUS-2026-08-23-f-…`, which does not publish. Sweeping the citations
# instead would have regenerated the problem the following week, which is [ADR-0031]'s
# whole argument.
#
# What a reader loses: nothing they came for. README.md answers "what is this",
# CHANGELOG.md "what changed", the specs and guides are the product, and docs/STATUS.md
# "where does it stand" — all declared canonical and written for that reader.
docs/status

# ── 2026-09-19 — the 0.8.2 cut, measured by RUNNING the filter, not by reading the config ──
#
# Everything below was found by exporting HEAD to a throwaway tree, running the real
# release filter over it, and diffing the survivors against public
# `master`. That diff is 22 paths that are public today and are not in the filtered
# tree, and NOT ONE of them was declared — so the public-regression gate was going to
# fail closed on this cut. It is fail-closed by design and it was right.
#
# Four of the 22 are genuine withdrawals and 18 are lifecycle moves. They are declared
# separately below because they are different claims, and collapsing them would hide
# the four behind the eighteen.

