# ── The 18 are the proposal lifecycle, not withdrawals ──
#
# Every one is a proposal that FOLDED and moved `active/` → `implemented/`, which is the
# L3 discipline working: a partial fold stays active, a complete one moves. Against the
# published tree a move is a deletion plus an addition and `public-regress` sees only the
# deletion, so all 18 read as vanishing. All 18 were verified present under
# `docs/proposals/implemented/` before this line was written — 18 moved, 0 absent — and
# `docs/proposals` is a `[[keep_tree]]`, so both halves publish and the reader loses
# nothing.
#
# A PREFIX and not 18 filenames, for the reason `canon.go` already gives against per-file
# declarations: a proposal folds most weeks, and a list that needs an edit at every fold is
# a permanent public-surface commitment made to work around a lifecycle. The prefix says
# "a document may leave `active/`", which is the only way a document ever leaves `active/`.
#
# ⚠ STATED RESIDUE, because a prefix is a real loosening: this would also wave through a
# proposal DELETED from `active/` rather than moved. Nothing gates the counterpart add —
# `spec ledger`'s `proposal-state-mismatch` runs the other direction (folded work left
# sitting in `active/`). So the check is the one run by hand above, and it is one command:
#   comm -23 <(git ls-tree -r --name-only <public> | sort) <(filtered tree | sort)
# then confirm each removed `active/` path exists under `implemented/`. Re-run it at each
# cut; it is the whole audit and it took under a minute.
docs/proposals/active
